message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Kintsugi: Joint Attention for Byte-Size Models — Shared Goals in Multi-Agent Systems Through Verified Uptake and Visible Repair"
authors:
  - family-names: Porter
    given-names: Matthew A.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0302-4812"
    affiliation: "Qompass AI"
version: "v2026-10-16"
date-released: 2026-10-16
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/qompassai/kintsugi"
keywords:
  - joint attention
  - multi-agent systems
  - small language models
  - LoRA
  - shared intentionality
  - agent coordination
abstract: "Multi-agent systems built from language models coordinate through text, but text is not attention: an agent can receive a goal statement without taking it up, and two agents can act on divergent readings of the same plan without either noticing. Kintsugi adapts the developmental-psychology mechanism of joint attention to byte-size local models. A deterministic protocol holds the commitment record — who holds focus, in what phase, under what contention — while small adapted models serve as the perceptual layer: each maintains a compressed goal-state and must produce a canonical uptake restatement before acting. Restatements are compared across agents and against the record; divergence is surfaced as a first-class repair event in the shared log rather than hidden, so the common ground accumulates visible seams — the gold lines of the repair. Authority never transfers to the models: they propose, the protocol disposes. We describe the design, a graduation ladder with demotion on factual failure, pilot evidence that the perceptual task is trainable in a small model (a LoRA adapter taking a granite-4.2-8b base from 0/20 to 19/20 clean held-out digests of the coordination log), and a pre-registered evaluation protocol for the shared-goal claim itself."
